{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Hasty pudding is a pudding or porridge of grains cooked in milk or water.","title":"Hasty pudding"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" In the United States, it often refers to a version made of ground corn.","title":"Hasty pudding"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Hasty pudding is notably mentioned in a verse of the early American song "Yankee Doodle".","title":"Hasty pudding"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":""Yankee Doodle" is a well-known British-American song, the early versions of which date back to before the Seven Years' War and the American Revolution (1775–83).","title":"Yankee Doodle"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is often sung patriotically in the United States today and is the state anthem of Connecticut.","title":"Yankee Doodle"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Its Roud Folk Song Index number is 4501.","title":"Yankee Doodle"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The melody is thought to be much older than both the lyrics and the subject, going back to folk songs of Medieval Europe.","title":"Yankee Doodle"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Renée Zellweger ( ; born April 25, 1969) is an American actress and producer.","title":"Renée Zellweger"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" She has received critical acclaim and many accolades, including an Academy Award, a BAFTA Award, three Golden Globe Awards, and three Screen Actors Guild Awards.","title":"Renée Zellweger"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" She was named Hasty Pudding's Woman of the Year in 2009, and established herself as one of the highest-paid Hollywood actresses in 2007.","title":"Renée Zellweger"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Capitol Hill Chorale is a 100-voice volunteer mixed choir with members throughout the Capitol Hill and Washington, DC metropolitan area.","title":"Capitol Hill Chorale"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Founded in 1993, the Chorale performs a principally classical repertoire with an emphasis on Eastern European liturgical works and early American song.","title":"Capitol Hill Chorale"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The William and Mary Quarterly (ISSN 0043-5597  ; ) is a quarterly history journal published by the Omohundro Institute of Early American History and Culture.","title":"William and Mary Quarterly"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The journal originated in 1892, making it one of the oldest scholarly journals in the United States.","title":"William and Mary Quarterly"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Currently in its third series, the Quarterly is the leading journal for the study of early American history and culture.","title":"William and Mary Quarterly"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It ranges chronologically from Old World–New World contacts to about 1820.","title":"William and Mary Quarterly"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Geographically, it focuses on North America—from New France and the Spanish American borderlands to British America and the Caribbean—and extends to Europe and West Africa.","title":"William and Mary Quarterly"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Though grounded in history, it welcomes works from all disciplines bearing on the early American period—for example, literature, law, political science, anthropology, archaeology, material culture, cultural studies.","title":"William and Mary Quarterly"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Stephen Nissenbaum (A.B. Harvard College, 1961; M.A. Columbia University, 1963; Ph.D., University of Wisconsin–Madison, 1968 ), is Professor Emeritus of the History Department, University of Massachusetts at Amherst, specializing in early American history through to the nineteenth century.","title":"Stephen Nissenbaum"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Most notably, he co-authored a book with Paul Boyer in 1974 about the Salem witch trials, "Salem Possessed: The Social Origins of Witchcraft", called "a landmark in early American studies" by John Putnam Demos.","title":"Stephen Nissenbaum"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Connecticut
